<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.14.7: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00319</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.14.7: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>a17a8484-7cc7-48f1-b301-b6c45ac0f4b9</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<para><quote>In questions 1 - 5, choose an equation of a line parallel to the given line and contains the given point. The choices are in slope-intercept form.</quote></para><section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="909f5114-1beb-4172-83b5-3cfc322d081c" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Line \(y = 4x + 2\)
          Point
          \((1, 2)\) </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(y=4x+2\)</para><para>\(y=4x-2\)</para><para>\(y=-\frac14x-2\)</para><para>\(y=-\frac14x+2\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(y=4x-2\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.14.2.</para><para>The correct answer is \(y = 4 x -2\). </para><para>The slope of a line parallel to \(y = 4x + 2\) is 4. The line contains the point (1,2).</para><para>\(\begin{aligned}y − y_1 &amp;= m(x − x_1)\\
        y − (2) &amp;= 4(x − (1))\\
        y − 2 &amp;= 4(x − 1)\\
        y − 2 &amp;= 4x − 4\\
        y &amp;= 4x − 2
        \end{aligned}\)</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="61d898ac-c69c-4b32-8003-4768a55fa342" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Line \(y = -3x -1\)
          Point \((2, -3)\) </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(y=-3x +3\)</para><para>\(y=-3x-3\)</para><para>\(y=\frac13x+3\)</para><para>\(y=\frac13x-3\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(y=-3x +3\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.14.2.</para><para>The correct answer is \(y = -3x +3\). </para><para>The slope of a line parallel to \(y = −3x − 1\) is \(−3\). The line contains the point (2,−3).</para><para>\(\begin{aligned}y − y_1 &amp;= m(x − x_1)\\
        y − (-3) &amp;= −3(x − (2))\\
        y + 3 &amp;= −3(x − 2)\\
        y +3 &amp;= −3x + 6\\
        y &amp;= −3x +3
        \end{aligned}\)</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="39322255-26c6-4b83-98b0-7e16753f6a4c" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Line \(2x - y = 6\)
          Point \((3, 0)\) </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(y=2x\)</para><para>\(y=-2x+6\)</para><para>\(y=-2x\)</para><para>\(y=2x-6\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(y=2x-6\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.14.2.</para><para>The correct answer is \(y=2x-6\). </para><para>The slope of a line parallel to \(2x −y = 6\) is 2. The line contains the point (3,0).</para><para>\(\begin{aligned}y − y_1 &amp;= m(x − x_1)\\
        y − (0) &amp;= 2(x − (3))\\
        y &amp;= 2(x − 3)\\
        y &amp;= 2x − 6\\
        \end{aligned}\)</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="72376f8b-6771-4aa4-8484-40c9d04b1f19" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Line \(x = -4\)
          Point \((-3, -5)\) </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(x=-3\)</para><para>\(y=-5\)</para><para>\(x=-5\)</para><para>\(y=-4\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(x=-3\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.14.4.</para><para>The correct answer is \(x=-3\). </para><para> The slope of a line parallel to \(x = −4\) is undefined. The line contains the point (−3,−5).
        The only equation with an undefined slope passing through (−3,−5) is \(x = −3\).
        The only equation with an undefined slope passing through (−3,−5) is \(x = −3\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="e11b16ef-f53c-40c9-bf63-f5f54930aa72" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Line \(y = 5\)
          Point \((2, -2)\) </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(x=2\)</para><para>\(x=5\)</para><para>\(y=-2\)</para><para>\(y=2\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(y=-2\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.14.4.</para><para>The correct answer is \(y=-2\). </para><para>The slope of a line parallel to \(y = 5\) is 0. The line contains the point (2,−2).
        The only equation with a slope of 0 passing through (2, −2) is \(y = −2\).
        The only equation with a slope of 0 passing through (2, −2) is \(y = −2\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="5980b1f7-cf0a-4fbc-94a4-7a42d531e127" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para><quote>In questions 6 – 10, find an equation of a line that is perpendicular to the given line and contains the given point. The choices are in slope-intercept form.</quote></para><para>Line \(y = -2x +3\)
          Point \((2, 2)\) </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(y=-2x+6\)</para><para>\(y=-\frac12x+3\)</para><para>\(y=\frac12x+3\)</para><para>\(y=\frac12x+1\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(y=\frac12x+1\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.14.3.</para><para>The correct answer is \(y=\frac12x+1\). </para><para>TThe slope of a line perpendicular to \(y = −2x + 3\) is \(\frac12\). The line contains the point (2,2).</para><para>\(\begin{aligned}y − y_1 &amp;= m(x − x_1)\\
        y − (2) &amp;= \frac12(x − (2))\\
        y −2 &amp;= \frac12(x − 2)\\
        y −2 &amp;= \frac12x − 1\\
        y &amp;= \frac12x + 1
        \end{aligned}\)</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="34f5f688-d8a6-44c9-ad20-c0035d7de4f2" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Line \(y = -x +5\)
          Point \((3, 3)\) </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(y=-x+6\)</para><para>\(y=x\)</para><para>\(y=x+5\)</para><para>\(y=-x+3\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(y=x\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.14.3.</para><para>The correct answer is \(y=x\). </para><para>The slope of a line perpendicular to \(y = −x + 5\) is 1. The line contains the point (3,3).</para><para>\(\begin{aligned}y − y_1 &amp;= m(x − x_1)\\
        y − (3) &amp;= 1(x − (3))\\
        y − 3 &amp;= 1(x − 3)\\
        y − 3 &amp;= x − 3\\
        y &amp;= x \\
        \end{aligned}\)</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="3854c9ff-58ec-4025-9fbb-4ee11fad4cae" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Line \(y = \frac{3}{4}x - 2\)
          Point \((-3, 4)\) </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(y=-\frac43x\)</para><para>\(y=-\frac43x+4\)</para><para>\(y=\frac34x+4\)</para><para>\(y=\frac34x+\frac{17}4\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(y=-\frac43x\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.14.3.</para><para>The correct answer is \(y=-\frac43x\). </para><para> The slope of a line perpendicular to \(y = \frac34x − 2\) is \(−\frac43.\) The line contains the point (−3,4).</para><para>\(\begin{aligned}y − y_1 &amp;= m(x − x_1)\\
        y − (4) &amp;= −\frac43(x − (−3))\\
        y −4 &amp;= −\frac43(x + 3)\\
        y −4 &amp;= −\frac43x − 4\\
        y &amp;= −\frac43x
        \end{aligned}\)</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="69dbb715-5c57-4a0a-b43b-7937bcb9423c" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Line \(x = -5\)
          Point \((1, -2)\) </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(y=-5\)</para><para>\(x=1\)</para><para>\(y=-2\)</para><para>\(x=-2\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(y=-2\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.14.4.</para><para>The correct answer is \(y=-2\). </para><para>The slope of a line perpendicular to \(x = −5\) is 0. The line contains the point (1,−2).
        The only equation with a slope of 0 passing through (1,−2) is y \(= −2\).
        The only equation with a slope of 0 passing through (1,−2) is y \(= −2\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="61146fd3-06de-4520-a2b4-fc2d65f3e87d" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Line \(y - 3 = 0\)
          Point \((-2, -4)\) </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(y=33\)</para><para>\(y=-4\)</para><para>\(x=3\)</para><para>\(x=-2\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(x=-2\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.14.4.</para><para>The correct answer is \(x=-2\). </para><para>The slope of a line parallel to \(y −3 = 0\) is undefined. The line contains the point (−2,−4).
        The only equation with an undefined slope passing through (−2,−4) is \(x = −2\).
        The only equation with an undefined slope passing through (−2,−4) is \(x = −2\).</para></solution></exercise></section></section>
